;;; The **M1 alive-set invariant**, broken on purpose — M1e S1e.3.3, `ST-M1`.
;;;
;;; ── The premise this refutes ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; `docs/kernel/inference/README.md` § M1 invariant:
;;;
;;;   1. No new objects. Rules don't `:assert` facts whose args introduce
;;;      names that weren't already in the ontology / facts.
;;;   … Under these clauses, every admissible hypothesis is enumerable from
;;;   the current KB state; deeper branches **eliminate** candidates, never
;;;   extend the space.
;;;
;;; The blind enumerator builds its candidate objects out of `kb.names()`,
;;; which is a function of the KB's **facts**. So a rule asserting a constant
;;; no fact mentions grows that pool inside a fork — while the lattice
;;; enumerates subsets of the `alive` set computed at *root*, where the name
;;; did not exist. The extended candidates are unreachable and nothing says so.
;;;
;;; ── What this program does ─────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;;   objects at root : A, B          (from `(p A B)`)
;;;   alive₀          : (q A B), (q B A)
;;;
;;;   `spawn` turns any committed `q` into `(p ?b Z)` — and **Z is a name no
;;;           fact has**, so it enters the KB only inside a fork.
;;;   `no-a` / `no-b` refute every `q` that points at A or at B, which is both
;;;           elements of alive₀ and nothing else.
;;;
;;; Every subset of alive₀ therefore dies, and the engine answers
;;;
;;;   solutions (k)   0     exhausted = true
;;;   verdict         No solution — the constraints are contradictory
;;;
;;; **which is false.** `{(q A Z), (q B Z)}` saturates to a consistent,
;;; quiescent, complete state — a model — and the search cannot reach it,
;;; because neither element was ever in `alive₀`. The refutation is not
;;; qualified by a cap: `exhausted = true` is the strong claim, and it is
;;; wrong.
;;;
;;; ── Why the lookahead is off ───────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; The flag chooses **which** wrong answer this file gives, and the sharper
;;; one is worth banking. With pre-branch lookahead on, the kill cache refutes
;;; both candidates before layer 1 and empties `alive`, so root itself is
;;; recorded and the run says `Solution k = 1` over a model with no `q` at all
;;; — a wrong *count* (1 where there are 2) rather than a wrong verdict. With
;;; it off, each singleton is entered and dies, and `k = 0, exhausted = true`
;;; is a **refutation**: a positive claim that no model exists.
;;;
;;; Deliberately **no `(absent …)` anywhere.** The obvious encoding of "Z must
;;; be reached" is a NAF guard, and it works — but it would also give this
;;; file the shape of M1e [Q-M1e.9](../../plans/m1e_review_processing/open_questions.md),
;;; where `dead` is not upward-closed under `absent`, and a reader could not
;;; then tell which of the two mechanisms cost the answer. Two positive
;;; refutation rules confound nothing.
;;;
;;; ── The control ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; [`alive-set-fresh-name-declared.ein`](alive-set-fresh-name-declared.ein)
;;; is this file plus **one fact**, `(seen Z)`, which changes no rule and no
;;; constraint and does nothing but put `Z` in the name universe at root. It
;;; answers `Solution k = 1` over the model above. One fact is the whole
;;; difference between a puzzle and a false refutation — and the same fact is
;;; what makes the program *conform*, since putting the name in the ontology
;;; is exactly what clause 1 asks for.
;;;
;;; ── Status ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
;;;
;;; **Not fixed, detected.** `ein_infer::invariant::Universe::rule_breaches`
;;; reports `new-object: 'Z', asserted by rule 'spawn'` — at load, over the
;;; program rather than over a run, so it answers for every run the program
;;; could have. `ein-infer/tests/alive_invariant.rs` holds the corpus's whole
;;; breach set, this file included; `--events` narrates it as a `warn` line.
;;; What would make a violation *unrepresentable* is the docs' F5 typed form,
;;; which has no schedule.
;;;
;;; `:expect` states **today's answer** and is meant to break, the same
;;; tripwire `naf-upward-closure.ein` and `alive-empty-*.ein` carry.

(config :enable-pre-branch-lookahead false)

(relation p T T)
(relation q T T)

(p A B)

(rule spawn ()
  :match  (q ?a ?b)
  :assert (p ?b Z)
  :why    "committing to (q {?a} {?b}) names Z, which no fact does")

(rule no-a ()
  :match  (q ?x A)
  :assert (false)
  :why    "nothing may reach A")

(rule no-b ()
  :match  (q ?x B)
  :assert (false)
  :why    "nothing may reach B")

(query
  :goal   (q ?x ?y)
  :no-hypothesis (p)
  ;; Today's answer. The truth is `(model (q A Z) (q B Z))`, which the
  ;; control reaches and this file's lattice cannot enumerate.
  :expect (false))
